  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA shortwave is currently tracking over the northern forecast \narea overnight. This should exit along with its respective \nprecip by dawn. Otherwise, the upper level pattern will continue\nto feature a trough over the central CONUS as the ridge over \nthe east remains in place but begins to weaken as it shifts to \nthe east- southeast with time. The now closed low at the base of\nthe trough over the central plains now opens and ejects a \nshortwave into the Lower Ohio Valley. This will bring a cold \nfront to the region entering the Upper OH Valley by Thursday \nnight. \n\nThe main focus will be on the front attempting to move through \nthe region from the northwest. This will track east through the \novernight and into the day on Friday. The rainfall threat will\nnot be impactful as ensembles have been lessing or holding on \nprecip amounts between 0.25\" and 0.50 on total rainfall. The NBM\nsuggest rainfall amounts will not be too concerning as the \nprobabilities of exceeding only 0.50\" will be between 30% and \n50% across the area. As well, the thunderstorm threat on \nThursday night and into Friday will be minimal as even the NBM \ngives a 10% chance of even reaching 500 J/Kg of SB CAPE. Even \nthe prob thunder for this period is only between 10% to 15%. \nThus, nothing is worth mentioning in the HWO at this time. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nModel ensembles indicate broad upper troughing should persist\nacross the NE CONUS from the weekend into the middle of next\nweek. A southern stream shortwave could bring a few showers to\nareas south of PIT on Sunday, otherwise mainly dry and\nseasonably cool weather is expected through the period as\nsurface high pressure builds under the broad trough by early\nnext week.",
